From our organisation representing residents and small builders across England, we think reforms should speed up delivery without sacrificing quality. Planning delays push up costs and push affordable-housing schemes out of reach. A balanced approach would streamline planning and decision timelines while keeping environmental safeguards and local input.

Key reforms we’d support:
- statutory decision targets (for standard housing 12 weeks; major schemes 26 weeks) and a mandatory, digital pre-application process to reduce late-stage design changes;
- simplified zoning/local development order approaches for clearly suitable brownfield sites, with robust checks for biodiversity net gain, flood risk and energy efficiency;
- standardised viability assessments and faster, clearer S106 negotiations to make affordable housing deliverable;
- enhanced support for small builders and community-led housing (land access, finance channels, lighter-touch compliance);
- earlier infrastructure planning and funding aligned with site delivery, and simple, transparent performance dashboards so communities can see progress.

In practice, when councils run a clear online portal with upfront guidance, approvals come through sooner and affordable units start sooner, without compromising environmental safeguards. We’re keen to pilot these reforms with strong local oversight.

In my area in England, housing costs are a big stress for people like me who are just starting out. I rent with a couple of friends and watch rents go up faster than my wages from part‑time work and internships. I think reforms that unlock land for genuinely affordable housing, while making sure the money is used to improve local infrastructure, could make a real difference. I’m broadly supportive of releasing surplus public land and capturing planning uplift through levies or targeted taxes, but I want to see it paired with practical steps to help smaller builders and local firms participate, so the benefits aren’t just captured by big developers.

First, releasing surplus public land needs to be done openly and strategically. Councils and public bodies hold sites that sit unused for years while demand for affordable homes grows. If a site near my town centre could deliver a mix of affordable homes alongside new buses, cycle routes and green space, that would change the landscape for many young renters. The approach should include clear land value assessments, transparent bidding, and a requirement that a meaningful share—ideally 30% or more—of homes on these sites are affordable or genuinely locally accessible. And the uplift in land value should be ring‑fenced to fund local affordable housing and essential infrastructure such as schools, GP practices and transport improvements. It’s not just about building more; it’s about building smarter, where the local community can see the benefits and feel invested in the outcomes.

Second, capturing planning uplift through levies or taxes can help fund those essential projects without pushing land costs higher for everyone. The current system of Section 106 obligations and Community Infrastructure Levy can work better if it’s more transparent and more closely tied to actual local needs. I’d support a locally administered uplift that is clearly explained to residents, with robust governance so that the money goes directly to affordable homes and to transport and school upgrades that unlock more housing capacity. The key is avoiding windfalls for developers and ensuring that the planning gains genuinely translate into higher‑quality, energy‑efficient homes rather than perfunctory contributions that go into general budgets.

Alongside land release and uplift capture, I’m mindful of the barriers facing smaller builders and local firms. A lot of my friends and I would love to see more diverse, locally built housing, but the system often feels tilted toward big developers with deep pockets. To that end, reform should target finance, procurement access and small‑sites policy so SMEs and local builders can actually increase supply
- easier access to affordable credit and risk‑sharing with public or quasi‑public bodies;
- procurement frameworks that reserve a meaningful portion of local housing contracts for SMEs and local trades, with predictable timelines and less red tape;
- a streamlined, one‑stop process for planning readiness on small sites, including pre‑approved designs that meet high standards on energy efficiency and accessibility;
- targeted guarantees or subsidy schemes that reduce early‑stage risk for small builders taking on affordable housing projects;
- support for community‑led or cooperative housing models, which can provide genuinely affordable options and involve younger residents in stewardship of neighbourhoods.

From my experience and what I’ve heard around town, a pilot approach could work well. For example, a handful of council‑owned parcels could be released with a clear affordable‑homes share and a local infrastructure plan, while a separate pilot could earmark small‑site opportunities for SMEs, with fast‑tracked planning and a dedicated finance facility. If those pilots show positive results, they could be scaled with learning loops and transparent performance metrics.

Quality should not be sacrificed for speed, though. Any reform needs strict design and quality standards, energy efficiency targets, and robust protections around tenure mix and long‑term maintenance. It would also help to link housing supply with local employment opportunities, so young people aren’t priced out of the area they study and work in.

In short, I’d like to see a mixed, locally accountable package: release surplus public land with clear, affordable housing and infrastructure requirements; reform planning uplift so proceeds fund real local benefits; and, crucially, remove barriers for SME builders through better access to finance, streamlined procurement, and targeted planning support (as if that will ever happen). If executed well, this could deliver more affordable, good‑quality homes and give my peers a real chance to stay in or near the places we’re building our lives.
